{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789050292,"nonce":"8bLKPHF5itz93BnTkw7ql2mzMyo=","title":"threads on wasm-fibers fails about one run in eighteen","message":"The threads suite under ash:wasm-fibers fails intermittently with an uncaught exception. Seen once in CI (run 34483838821) and reproduced on the NUC.\n\n    FAIL ash:wasm-fibers bin/threads.hl  4019ms  exit 1: [ash]   at fun$857(?:1)\n\n**Rate.** 1 failure in 18 runs through the conformance harness on the NUC. ZERO in 30 runs of the same module invoked directly (`ash-wasm-run bin/threads.wasm-fibers.wasm` from a shell). CI has passed it twice and failed it once. So the harness path is the reproducer, not the module on its own -- piped stdio, the timeout wrapper and back-to-back process churn are the candidate differences, and one of them perturbs the timing enough to expose it.\n\n**It dies partway, not at teardown.** The failing run took 4019ms against ~6850ms for every passing one, and the suite is 110 assertions that otherwise all pass.\n\n**Ruled out, with the experiment for each:**\n\n- *Caused by sqlite.wasm now existing* (it appeared in the first CI run after that step landed): NO. threads/wasm-fibers passes 110/110 on the NUC with the side module present.\n- *Tied to rebuilding a stale module*: NO. Deleting bin/threads.wasm-fibers.wasm and running twice -- the rebuilding run and the cached run -- both pass.\n- *CI-specific*: NO. It reproduces on the NUC.\n\n**What the message actually is, nobody knows yet.** `at fun$857(?:1)` is a stack FRAME. The line naming the exception sits above it and the harness was reporting only the last line. Fixed in 06f09eb8, so the next occurrence carries `uncaught exception: \u003cwhat\u003e`. Re-run and read that before theorising about the fiber scheduler.\n\nRelated but separate: ash:wasm (no fibers) TIMEOUTs on this suite, long-standing.","files":null}]}